    <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 pb-12">
        <article class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-md overflow-hidden">
            <div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full overflow-hidden bg-gray-50">
                <img src="/blog/assets/img/featured/dengue-recovery-time.jpg"
                     alt="Woman resting in bed with warm tea, showing the prolonged fatigue that follows dengue fever recovery"
                     width="1600" height="900" class="w-full h-full object-cover">
            </div>

            <div class="p-6 md:p-10 border-b border-gray-100">
                <span class="inline-block bg-secondary-600 text-white text-xs font-semibold px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Internal Medicine · Monsoon Health</span>
                <h1 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-4">Fever Gone, Still Exhausted: What Dengue Recovery Actually Looks Like</h1>
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
                    <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><i data-feather="calendar" class="w-4 h-4"></i> September 2, 2026</span>
                    <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><i data-feather="clock" class="w-4 h-4"></i> 9 min read</span>
                    <span class="flex items-center gap-1"><i data-feather="user" class="w-4 h-4"></i> Reviewed by the Internal Medicine Team, Raj Hospitals Ranchi</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="p-6 md:p-10">

                <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">
                    Every year, once the monsoon starts pulling back from Jharkhand, our outpatient department fills with a very particular kind of worried family. The dengue fever broke four days ago. The reports are improving. And yet the patient cannot climb a flight of stairs without sitting down. Parents ask the same question in slightly different words: <em>if the fever is gone, why is he still like this?</em> The short answer is that <strong>dengue recovery time is much longer than dengue fever time</strong>, and the gap between the two is where most of the unnecessary panic lives.
                </p>

                <div class="raj-callout raj-callout-info">
                    <strong class="text-secondary-600">Quick answer:</strong>
                    <span class="text-gray-700"> The fever usually settles within 5 to 7 days. The exhaustion, poor appetite and heavy-limb feeling typically last another <strong>1 to 2 weeks</strong>, and mild tiredness can linger for 4 to 6 weeks. That is normal. What is <em>not</em> normal is severe abdominal pain, repeated vomiting, bleeding, breathlessness or a fever that returns. Those need same-day review at our <a href="/specialties/emergency" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">24x7 emergency department in Ranchi</a>.</span>
                </div>

                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="activity" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> Why Fatigue Outlasts the Fever by One to Two Weeks</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Dengue is not a disease where the virus does most of the damage. Your immune system does. When the dengue virus enters the bloodstream, the body floods itself with signalling chemicals called cytokines, chiefly interleukin-6, interleukin-10 and tumour necrosis factor alpha. These are what actually produce the high fever, the crushing headache behind the eyes, and the deep muscle and bone pain that earned dengue its old name, breakbone fever.
                </p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Here is the part patients are rarely told: <strong>the virus clears from the blood before the cytokines do.</strong> By the time your temperature chart flattens, the virus itself is largely gone, but the inflammatory chemicals continue circulating for days. They act directly on skeletal muscle, on the mitochondria that generate energy inside your cells, and on the brain regions that regulate alertness and motivation. That is why the tiredness feels different from ordinary tiredness. It is not laziness or weakness of will. It is measurable, biological, and it has a predictable end point.
                </p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Layer on top of that what actually happened during the illness. Most dengue patients eat almost nothing for four or five days. They lose fluid through fever and sweating. Muscle protein is broken down for energy. Sleep is fragmented by fever spikes. Even a perfectly healthy 25-year-old would feel wrecked after a week of that without any virus involved. Combine the two and a recovery window of ten to fourteen days stops being surprising. Doctors call this pattern <strong>post-dengue fatigue</strong>, and it is one of the most consistently reported after-effects of the illness worldwide.
                </p>

                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="calendar" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> The Honest Dengue Recovery Timeline</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Dengue moves through three recognised phases. Knowing which one you are in tells you far more than any single lab value.
                </p>

                <div class="raj-grid">
                    <div class="raj-card">
                        <h3 class="raj-card-title"><i data-feather="thermometer" class="raj-icon raj-icon-md raj-icon-red"></i> Days 1&ndash;3: Febrile phase</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">High fever, severe headache, pain behind the eyes, body and joint ache, sometimes a flushed rash. This phase feels the worst but is usually the least dangerous. Paracetamol and fluids are the mainstay.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="raj-card">
                        <h3 class="raj-card-title"><i data-feather="alert-triangle" class="raj-icon raj-icon-md raj-icon-yellow"></i> Days 4&ndash;6: Critical phase</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">The fever starts falling and families relax. This is exactly the wrong moment to relax. Plasma can leak from small blood vessels for 24 to 48 hours here, and platelets hit their lowest point. Almost every serious dengue complication happens in this window.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="raj-card">
                        <h3 class="raj-card-title"><i data-feather="trending-up" class="raj-icon raj-icon-md raj-icon-green"></i> Days 7&ndash;10: Recovery phase</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">Leaked fluid is reabsorbed, appetite flickers back, platelets climb. A distinctive itchy red rash on the palms and soles, sometimes called the rash of convalescence, often appears here and is a good sign.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="raj-card">
                        <h3 class="raj-card-title"><i data-feather="battery-charging" class="raj-icon raj-icon-md raj-icon-blue"></i> Weeks 2&ndash;6: Fatigue tail</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">Blood counts are normal but stamina is not. Expect reduced exercise tolerance, low mood, poor concentration and, from around week 6, some temporary hair shedding. All of this resolves.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="raj-callout raj-callout-warning mt-6">
                    <strong class="text-amber-700">The single most important sentence in this article:</strong>
                    <span class="text-gray-700"> in dengue, the danger period begins <em>as the fever comes down</em>, not when it is at its peak. A family that stops monitoring on day 4 because "the fever has gone" is switching off the alarm at exactly the wrong hour. If you are in day 4 to 6 of illness, keep watching, keep hydrating, and keep the <a href="/contact" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">Raj Hospitals emergency contact number</a> saved on your phone.</span>
                </div>

                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="bar-chart-2" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> What a Normal Platelet Recovery Curve Looks Like</h2>

                <figure class="my-6">
                    <img src="/blog/assets/img/dengue-platelet-count-blood-test.jpg"
                         alt="Blood sample tubes used for the daily platelet count and haematocrit monitoring that guides dengue treatment"
                         width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-sm">
                    <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">Daily platelet counts matter far less as a single number than as a trend line. Our <a href="/specialties/laboratory-investigations" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline">in-house laboratory in Ranchi</a> reports dengue profiles the same day.</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    No number in Indian medicine causes more household anxiety than the platelet count. Families track it like a cricket score, and a drop from 90,000 to 70,000 can trigger a midnight hospital run. So let us be precise about what actually happens.
                </p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Platelets begin falling from about day 3, reach their lowest point somewhere between <strong>day 4 and day 7</strong>, and then start climbing. The rise is usually steady and takes three to five days to reach the normal range. In most uncomplicated cases the count is back above 150,000 within seven to ten days of the fever settling. Two things doctors watch that patients usually do not:
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
                    <li><strong>The direction, not the value.</strong> A count of 60,000 that rose from 45,000 yesterday is a better situation than a count of 90,000 that fell from 140,000. Trend beats snapshot every time.</li>
                    <li><strong>The haematocrit alongside it.</strong> A rising haematocrit with a falling platelet count is the classic signature of plasma leakage, and it is the finding that decides whether someone needs admission. This is why a full dengue profile with a <a href="/blog/haemoglobin-kya-hai/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">complete blood count including haemoglobin and haematocrit</a> is more useful than a standalone platelet test.</li>
                    <li><strong>Whether there is any bleeding.</strong> Bleeding, not the number, drives the decision to transfuse. A patient with a count of 20,000 and no bleeding is often managed with observation alone, while a patient with active bleeding may need intervention at a higher count.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    A related caution: papaya leaf extract, kiwi, goat milk and a long list of home remedies circulate every dengue season in Jharkhand. None of them is a substitute for fluid monitoring, and none of them changes the natural platelet curve in a way that has been reliably demonstrated. They are usually harmless, but a family that trusts them instead of returning for a review is taking a real risk. Our physicians would far rather you take both, than take one and skip the hospital.
                </p>

                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="alert-octagon" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> Genuine Red Flags: When to Come Back Immediately</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    These are the internationally recognised dengue warning signs. Any one of them justifies an immediate visit, at any hour, without waiting for morning.
                </p>
                <div class="raj-callout raj-callout-danger">
                    <strong class="text-red-700">Go to the emergency department now if there is:</strong>
                    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-700 mt-2">
                        <li><strong>Severe or persistent abdominal pain</strong>, particularly in the upper right side or around the stomach</li>
                        <li><strong>Repeated vomiting</strong> &mdash; three or more episodes in an hour, or an inability to keep fluids down</li>
                        <li><strong>Bleeding</strong> from gums or nose, blood in vomit, black tarry stools, or unusually heavy menstrual bleeding</li>
                        <li><strong>Breathlessness</strong> or difficulty lying flat, which can indicate fluid collecting around the lungs</li>
                        <li><strong>Cold, clammy hands and feet</strong> with a fast weak pulse &mdash; an early sign of circulatory shock</li>
                        <li><strong>Drowsiness, confusion, or unusual irritability and restlessness</strong>, especially in children and elderly patients</li>
                        <li><strong>Passing very little urine</strong> &mdash; less than about four times in 24 hours in an adult</li>
                        <li><strong>Fever that returns</strong> after having settled for a day or more</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 mt-6">
                    Two groups deserve a lower threshold for coming in. Children may not describe abdominal pain clearly and often present with irritability, refusal to feed and lethargy instead, which is why our <a href="/specialties/pediatrics-and-neonatology" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">paediatrics and neonatology team</a> assesses paediatric dengue differently from adult dengue. And patients with diabetes, kidney disease, heart failure or those on blood thinners have far less physiological margin, so they are usually monitored more closely and admitted earlier. If you have <a href="/blog/diabetes-ke-lakshan-aur-upchar-control-karne-ke-tarike/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">diabetes along with dengue</a>, sugar control tends to swing wildly during the illness and needs supervision.
                </p>

                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="clipboard" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> When to Come Back to Raj Hospitals for a Review</h2>

                <figure class="my-6">
                    <img src="/blog/assets/img/dengue-follow-up-doctor-review.jpg"
                         alt="Doctor reviewing medication and recovery progress with a patient during a post-dengue follow-up consultation"
                         width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-sm">
                    <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">A single follow-up visit after the fever settles catches the complications that home monitoring misses.</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Even when everything is going well, we ask dengue patients to come back at least once. A short review appointment is not a formality; it is where slow-burning problems get caught.
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
                    <li><strong>Day 3 to 5 of illness, daily.</strong> During the critical phase, a daily platelet count and haematocrit with a quick clinical check is standard. Most patients do this as a walk-in and go home.</li>
                    <li><strong>Two to three days after the fever settles.</strong> One repeat blood count to confirm platelets are climbing and haematocrit is stable.</li>
                    <li><strong>At two weeks, if fatigue is not improving at all.</strong> This is when we look for the things dengue can unmask or leave behind: <a href="/blog/danger-level-of-sgpt-and-sgot/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">raised liver enzymes such as SGPT and SGOT</a>, which are common in dengue and usually settle on their own; anaemia; low thyroid function; or a second infection layered on top.</li>
                    <li><strong>Any time the picture does not fit.</strong> Dengue, <a href="/blog/chikungunya-ke-lakshan-in-hindi/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">chikungunya</a>, <a href="/blog/typhoid-bar-bar-kyu-hota-hai/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">typhoid</a>, malaria, scrub typhus and <a href="/blog/lungs-infection-symptoms-in-hindi/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">chest infections</a> all circulate together in a Jharkhand monsoon and can look alike in the first three days. A fever that behaves unusually deserves a fresh look rather than an assumption.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Patients who deteriorate are managed by our <a href="/specialties/critical-care" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">critical care unit in Ranchi</a>, where fluid resuscitation in severe dengue is a precise, hour-by-hour exercise rather than simply running drips fast. Getting that balance wrong in either direction is what turns a survivable illness into a dangerous one, and it is the main reason severe dengue belongs in hospital rather than at home.
                </p>

                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="droplet" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> Hydration, Rest and Diet Through the Recovery Window</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    There is no antiviral for dengue. Everything that helps is supportive, which makes the basics unusually important.
                </p>
                <div class="raj-grid">
                    <div class="raj-card">
                        <h3 class="raj-card-title"><i data-feather="coffee" class="raj-icon raj-icon-md raj-icon-blue"></i> Fluids: aim for 2.5 to 3 litres a day</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-2">Spread it out rather than drinking large volumes at once. Oral rehydration solution, coconut water, buttermilk, thin dal water, rice kanji and clear soups all count and add electrolytes plain water does not.</p>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">Use urine as the gauge: pale and passed at least four to six times a day means you are keeping up. Dark, scanty urine means you are behind. Our guide to <a href="/blog/dehydration-symptoms-in-hindi/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline">recognising dehydration early</a> covers the signs in more detail.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="raj-card">
                        <h3 class="raj-card-title"><i data-feather="x-circle" class="raj-icon raj-icon-md raj-icon-red"></i> Painkillers: paracetamol only</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">Avoid ibuprofen, diclofenac, aspirin, nimesulide and combination pain powders. These non-steroidal drugs increase bleeding risk and can irritate the stomach lining at precisely the moment platelets are lowest. Paracetamol at the dose your doctor advised is the safe choice.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="raj-card">
                        <h3 class="raj-card-title"><i data-feather="award" class="raj-icon raj-icon-md raj-icon-green"></i> Food: small, frequent, protein-forward</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">Appetite returns slowly, so six small meals beat three large ones. Prioritise dal, eggs, curd, paneer, khichdi and soft fruit. Protein and iron intake matter because rebuilding lost muscle is what actually ends the fatigue. Our <a href="/specialties/nutrition-and-dietetics" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline">dietetics team</a> can build a recovery plan for patients who are struggling to eat.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="raj-card">
                        <h3 class="raj-card-title"><i data-feather="moon" class="raj-icon raj-icon-md raj-icon-secondary"></i> Activity: return in steps, not in one jump</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">Add roughly 20 to 30 percent more activity every two days. Walking before jogging, desk work before field work, and no heavy gym training or contact sport for at least two weeks after the platelet count normalises. Pushing hard in week one reliably sets recovery back.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="shield" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> Do Not Let the Second Infection Catch You Off Guard</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    There are four dengue serotypes circulating in India. Recovering from one gives you lifelong immunity against that serotype and none at all against the other three. Worse, a second infection with a <em>different</em> serotype carries a measurably higher risk of severe dengue because of how antibodies from the first infection interact with the new virus. This is not a reason to panic, but it is a strong reason to act earlier next time rather than later.
                </p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    Practically, that means household mosquito control matters even after you have had dengue. The <em>Aedes</em> mosquito breeds in clean standing water and bites during daylight hours, which is why bed nets alone are not enough. Empty and scrub coolers weekly, cover overhead tanks, clear water from flower pot trays, discarded tyres and construction sites, and use repellent during the day. Our broader <a href="/blog/monsoon-health-tips-hindi/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">monsoon health precautions guide</a> and our note on <a href="/blog/immunity-kaise-badhaye/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">building immunity during the rainy season</a> cover the household side of prevention.
                </p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
                    One more thing worth knowing: a small number of patients develop a secondary bacterial infection during or shortly after dengue, which can look like a relapse. If a fever returns with rigors, focal pain or a rapidly worsening general condition, that pattern is closer to <a href="/blog/blood-infection-kaise-hota-hai/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">a bloodstream infection</a> than to dengue itself, and it needs assessment rather than waiting.
                </p>

