                <h2 class="raj-h2"><i data-feather="message-circle" class="raj-h2-icon"></i> Frequently Asked Questions About Dengue Recovery</h2>
                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">How long does dengue recovery take after the fever stops?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">The fever usually settles in 5 to 7 days, but full recovery takes longer. Most adults feel weak, low on appetite and easily tired for another 1 to 2 weeks after the temperature normalises. A smaller group carries mild fatigue and body ache for 4 to 6 weeks. This is expected and does not mean the infection is still active.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Why do I still feel exhausted after my dengue fever has gone?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Dengue triggers a strong immune response that releases inflammatory cytokines. These chemicals keep circulating for days after the virus itself is cleared, and they act on muscle and brain tissue to produce fatigue, body ache and poor concentration. Add several days of reduced food intake, dehydration and broken sleep, and the tiredness is fully explainable. It is called post-dengue fatigue and it resolves on its own.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">When does the platelet count go back to normal after dengue?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Platelets typically bottom out around day 4 to 7 of illness, often at the same time the fever starts breaking. The count then climbs back over the next 3 to 5 days and is usually normal within 7 to 10 days of the fever settling. A rising platelet count is one of the most reassuring signs that recovery is on track, which is why we track the trend rather than reacting to one reading.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">What are the danger signs after dengue fever that need immediate hospital care?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Return to hospital immediately for severe or persistent abdominal pain, repeated vomiting, bleeding from gums or nose, blood in vomit or stool, black tarry stools, breathlessness, cold and clammy hands and feet, drowsiness or irritability, reduced urine output, or a fever that returns after having settled. These warning signs usually appear as the fever breaks, not at its peak. Our <a href="/specialties/emergency" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">emergency department in Ranchi</a> runs 24x7 for exactly this.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Do I need a platelet transfusion if my count is low after dengue?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">In most cases, no. A low platelet count on its own is not a reason to transfuse. Doctors look at whether there is active bleeding and at how the patient is clinically doing, not just at the number. Transfusion is generally reserved for very low counts with significant bleeding, and unnecessary transfusion carries its own risks. This is a decision for your treating physician, not for a WhatsApp forward.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Is hair fall after dengue normal?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Noticeable hair shedding starting about 6 to 12 weeks after any severe febrile illness is a well-recognised pattern called telogen effluvium. The high fever pushes many follicles into a resting phase at the same time and they shed together. It is temporary and density recovers over the following months. If shedding is severe or continues past six months, a <a href="/blog/kya-dermatologist-baalon-ke-jhadne-ka-ilaj-kar-sakte-hain/" class="text-secondary-600 hover:underline font-semibold">dermatology review for hair fall</a> is worthwhile.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">What should I eat and drink during dengue recovery?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Aim for 2.5 to 3 litres of fluid a day for an average adult, spread through the day. Oral rehydration solution, coconut water, buttermilk, thin dal and soups all count. Eat small frequent meals with easily digestible protein such as dal, eggs, curd and paneer, since protein is what rebuilds lost strength. Avoid alcohol until liver enzymes normalise, and avoid painkillers other than paracetamol unless specifically advised.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">Can dengue come back a second time?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. There are four dengue serotypes, and recovering from one gives lifelong protection against that serotype only. A second infection with a different serotype carries a higher risk of severe dengue, so anyone who has had dengue before should seek review earlier rather than later during a new fever episode.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-800 mb-2">How soon can I return to work or the gym after dengue?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Most people manage desk work about a week after the fever settles, often at reduced hours initially. Physically demanding work and gym training should wait until the platelet count has normalised and then be reintroduced gradually over one to two weeks. Contact sports carry a bleeding risk while platelets are still recovering and should wait the longest.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

